revision.text:
'''陰陽寮'''（）は、[[内裏]]の機関のひとつ。[[中務省]]に属する占い・天文・漏刻・暦の編纂を担当する部署。

長官は陰陽頭で、陰陽師を束ねる。さらにそれぞれの分野に陰陽博士・天文博士・漏刻博士・暦博士がおり、その下には学生である得業生が学ぶ。大陰陽師・[[安倍晴明]]もこのうちの天文博士に任命されている。

{{history-stub}}

'''陰陽寮'''（おんみょうりょう）は、[[内裏]]の機関のひとつ。[[中務省]]に属する占い・天文・漏刻・暦の編纂を担当する部署。

長官は陰陽頭で、[[陰陽師]]を束ねる。さらにそれぞれの分野に陰陽博士・天文博士・漏刻博士・暦博士がおり、その下には学生である得業生が学ぶ。大陰陽師・[[安倍晴明]]もこのうちの天文博士に任命されている。

{{history-stub}}

{{Draft}}
Also, there was the "Five Dragons Festival/ritual" (''Goryūsai''. {{lang|ja|五龍祭}}) was performed by ''[[onmyōji]]'' or yin-yang masters.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} The oldest mention of this in literature is from ''[[Fusō Ryakuki]]'', entry of Engi 2/902AD, 17th day of the 6th moon.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} Sometimes the performance of the rain ritual by Esoteric Buddhists (''shōugyōhō'') would be followed in succession by the Five Dragons Ritual from the {{illm|Yin-Yang Bureau|ja|陰陽寮}}{{sfnb|Ruppert|2002|pp=157–158}} The Five Dragon rites performed by the ''[[onmyōji]]'' or yin yang masters had its heyday around the 10–11th centuries.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} There are ''[[mokkan]]'' or inscribed wooden tablets used in these rites that have been unearthed (e.g., from 8–10th century site, and a 9th-century site.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=6–7}}

In Japan there also developed a legend that the primordial being Banko ([[Pangu]] of Chinese myth) sired the Five Dragon Kings, who were invoked in the ritual texts or {{illm|saimon (liturgy)|lt=''saimon''|ja|祭文}} read in Shinto or Onmyōdō rites, but the five beings later began to be seen less as monsters and more as wise princes,{{sfnb|Faure|2005|pp=82–85}}

