{{nihongo|'''Onmyōji'''|陰陽師||literally: [[yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] master||lead=yes}} was one of the official positions belonging to the {{ill|Yin-Yang Bureau|lt=|ja|陰陽寮}} of the [[Ministry of the Center]] under the [[ritsuryō]] system in ancient Japan, and was assigned as a technical officer in charge of [[divination]] and geomorphology based on the theory of the [[Yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] [[Gogyo|five phases]]. In the [[middle ages]] and [[early modern period|early modern period]], the term was used to refer to those who performed prayers and divination in the private sector, and some of them were regarded as a kind of [[kannushi|clergy]].[[File:Tamamonomae_Onmyoji.jpg|right|thumb|300x300px|From the Nara picture book "[[Tamamo-no-Mae]]," published in the early [[Edo period]], depicting an onmyōji performing divination with [[counting rods]]. From the collection of the [[Kyoto University]] Library.]]Sometimes the performance of the rain ritual by Esoteric Buddhists (''shōugyōhō'') would be followed in succession by the Five Dragons Ritual from the {{illm|Yin-Yang Bureau|ja|陰陽寮}}{{sfnb|Ruppert|2002|pp=157–158}} {{nihongo|'''[[Onmyōji]]'''|陰陽師||literally: [[yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] master||lead=yes}} was one of the official positions belonging to the {{ill|Yin-Yang Bureau|lt=Bureau of Onmyō|ja|陰陽寮}}

== Excerpt ==
Also, there was the "Five Dragons Festival/ritual" (''Goryūsai''. {{lang|ja|五龍祭}}) was performed by ''[[onmyōji]]'' or yin-yang masters.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} The oldest mention of this in literature is from ''[[Fusō Ryakuki]]'', entry of Engi 2/902AD, 17th day of the 6th moon.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} Sometimes the performance of the rain ritual by Esoteric Buddhists (''shōugyōhō'') would be followed in succession by the Five Dragons Ritual from the {{illm|Yin-Yang Bureau|ja|陰陽寮}}{{sfnb|Ruppert|2002|pp=157–158}} The Five Dragon rites performed by the ''[[onmyōji]]'' or yin yang masters had its heyday around the 10–11th centuries.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=18–19}} There are ''[[mokkan]]'' or inscribed wooden tablets used in these rites that have been unearthed (e.g., from 8–10th century site, and a 9th-century site.{{sfnb|Monta|2012|pp=6–7}}

In Japan there also developed a legend that the primordial being Banko ([[Pangu]] of Chinese myth) sired the Five Dragon Kings, who were invoked in the ritual texts or {{illm|saimon (liturgy)|lt=''saimon''|ja|祭文}} read in Shinto or Onmyōdō rites, but the five beings later began to be seen less as monsters and more as wise princes,{{sfnb|Faure|2005|pp=82–85}}
==In Popular culture==
The same phrase is also used in [[The Yinyang Master]]
[[ja:陰陽寮]]

