{{Draft}}
{{ill|Yin-Yang Bureau|lt=Yin-Yang Bureau|ja|陰陽寮|en:draft|Yin-Yang Bureau}} was a bureau of the [[Ministry of the Center]] under the [[ritsuryō]] system in ancient Japan. {{nihongo|'''Onmyōji'''|陰陽師||literally: [[yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] master||lead=yes}} was one of the official positions belonging to it.  They were assigned as a technical officer in charge of [[divination]] and geomorphology based on the theory of the [[Yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] [[Gogyo|five phases]]. In the [[middle ages]] and [[early modern period]], the term was used to refer to those who performed prayers and divination in the private sector, and some of them were regarded as a kind of [[kannushi|clergy]].

Sometimes the performance of the rain ritual by Esoteric Buddhists (''shōugyōhō'') would be followed in succession by the Five Dragons Ritual from the {{illm|Yin-Yang Bureau|ja|陰陽寮}}{{sfnb|Ruppert|2002|pp=157–158}} {{nihongo|'''[[Onmyōji]]'''|陰陽師||literally: [[yin and yang|yin-and-yang]] master||lead=yes}} was one of the official positions belonging to the {{ill|User:Immanuelle/Yin-Yang Bureau|lt=Bureau of Onmyō|ja|陰陽寮}}
==In Popular culture==
The same phrase is also used in [[The Yinyang Master|The Yinyang Master]]
[[ja:陰陽寮]]

